  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nToday will focus on thunderstorm activity across the entire \narea which may become strong to severe and pose a flash flood \nrisk. Severe indices are elevated and in place to support severe\nthunderstorms. With CAPE close to near 3500 J/kg, enough \ninstability and the lack of CIN there will support strong \nthunderstorm development into the evening. There is more \nmoisture in the forecast soundings which will support wet \ndownburst/heavy downpours which can cause damaging wind, however\nflooding will be the main threat with PWATS close to 1.9 inches\nalong with the greater moisture in the column than yesterday. \nFreezing levels around 15K+ feet therefore hail will have a \nhard time reaching the ground before melting, however small hail\ncould be a possibility under severe storm activity, especially \nwithin a downburst. The area is mainly under a marginal risk for\nsevere thunderstorms into the evening issued by SPC.\n\nWith the lack of shear and steering flow aloft storms will have\na hard time to become severe although downburst potential will \nbe able to support flash flooding. If storms hover over the same\narea and/or backbuild and train areas will be susceptible to \nflooding issues, especially low lying and flood prone areas that\nare hit repeatedly. Storms are moving slightly faster than \nyesterday which will bode well for the area and to prevent \nsignificant flooding issues. We are under a marginal risk for \nexcessive rainfall from WPC so this does support isolated flash \nflood potential. \n\nHi-res models suggest activity continuing into the evening at \nwhich point will start to diminish around 6Z. The most \naggressive models are the Hi-resW FV3 and ARW who had convection\nstarting off early around 16Z which is spot on as the \nconvection is ongoing across the Ohio River currently well ahead\nof a cold front to the west, however the HRRR is quiet today \nwith some convection starting around 21Z and confined to mainly \nthe norther tiers our our CWA. This is not the case with a line \ncongealing from the top of our CWA along the Ohio River down to \nthe bottom of our CWA. Severe thunderstorms looks to become more\nisolated in nature today. If convection brings in enough cloud \ncoverage some of that high instability will be lost and will \nhelp limit thunderstorms or at least the potential for severe by\nlate afternoon across the CWA. More storm potential and the \npossibility of isolated severe thunderstorms will continue into \nthe new work week. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe heat wave is over and temperatures will fall back down to \naround seasonable for us through the new work week. This will \ndeny the possibility of any heat headlines, especially with the\nchances for convection on a daily basis. Also, cloud coverage \nseems fairly abundant for the afternoons and evenings through \nthe work week, therefore that should suppress the potential for\nabove average temperatures and the possibility of any heat \nheadlines such as an heat advisory. As high pressure exits the \narea it will be replaced by low pressure energy and a short wave\ntrough which models have sticking around across the area for \nthe duration of the new work week providing that potential for \ncloudy skies and convection during daytime heating.",
